#
# Per-radio calibration, in the attribute database format of attrdb(6).
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS.  Every number in it was measured on one board
# and is wrong on another.  os/inferno/stm32f407/spistm32f4.c carried
# ".ppb = -3550, measured on this board and not taken from anywhere",
# which was right when there was one board; with two, that line is
# wrong on half the bench, and the two crystals here are 64 parts per
# million apart -- 58.7kHz at 915MHz, wider than the 50kHz channel
# filter, so an uncorrected pair cannot hear each other even in
# principle.  Either every board gets its own kernel, which is a flash
# cycle per measurement and an image that is silently wrong on the
# board next door, or the image carries them all and each board picks
# its own.  radiocfg(1) is the picking.
#
# WHY uid IS THE KEY.  An identity a board is told is circular: the
# thing that would tell it is the thing that needs to know.  And the
# radio has no identity of its own -- a CC1120 answers PARTNUMBER 0x48
# and PARTVERSION 0x21 and nothing that distinguishes one part from
# another.  The microcontroller does: RM0090 39.1, a 96-bit factory
# serial, read-only, which no reflash can change.  #P/uid serves it as
# twenty-four hex digits, most significant word first.
#
# So the key is the BOARD and not the click, which is an approximation
# and the wrong one if a click is ever moved between boards, because
# the crystal belongs to the click.  Each entry says which click it was
# measured with so that a move is recordable rather than silent.
#
# This is not /lib/ndb/radio.  ndb(6) is the attribute database for
# network configuration and /lib/ndb/local names the files that make up
# that one logical database; a crystal error is not network data, and
# putting it there would have every program that opens /lib/ndb/local
# read it.  The format is attrdb(6) either way and attrdb(2) reads
# both.
#
# ATTRIBUTES.
#	uid		the board's #P/uid, the key
#	radio		which part, for a reader that serves more than one
#	board, click	what it was measured in and with
#	name		what the bench calls it
#	call		its AX.25 callsign: two stations answering to one
#			name is not a network, so this is per board too
#	peer		the callsign this station addresses.  #a matches an
#			incoming frame against a conversation by the
#			frame's SOURCE, so two stations both addressing
#			QST hear nothing from each other -- each needs to
#			name the other.  Who talks to whom is a fact about
#			a bench, so it is data here and not a constant in
#			an init.
#	txramp		PA_CFG2's PA_POWER_RAMP, 0 to 63, if this station
#			is not to transmit at the driver's default.  The
#			output is (ramp+1)/2 - 18 dBm, so 63 is +14 and 15
#			is -10.  It is here because a station's power
#			belongs to it like its callsign does, and because
#			it is the one knob that makes a link lossy on
#			purpose: a connected link on a half-duplex channel
#			self-coordinates and loses nothing, so REJ, RNR
#			and the retransmission counter are unreachable
#			until something costs frames.  See
#			doc/radio-plan.ms item 9.
#	role		"caller" or "answerer", for connected mode.  A
#			link has one of each and which board is which is a
#			bench fact too.  Absent means UI frames only: a
#			beacon needs no state machine and no peer to
#			answer, so that stays the default.
#	ppb		crystal error, parts per billion, for the ppm verb
#	rssioffset	dB, AGC_GAIN_ADJUST
#	csthr		dBm, AGC_CS_THR
#	rssislope	dB of RSSI per dB of transmitter, from radiocal(1)
#	slopesource	what measured the slope, because it matters
#	pend		0 to make the receive path FLUSH a frame whose
#			payload has not all arrived, which is what the
#			cc1120 driver did for months, instead of keeping
#			the length byte and finishing the frame when the
#			rest turns up.  Absent means 1, which is what a
#			station should run.
#
#			It is here, and not only in the driver, because
#			the claim that keeping is better has to be
#			measured against the old behaviour -- and running
#			the old behaviour from an OLD KERNEL compares
#			every other difference between two builds at the
#			same time.  This way the kernel is byte-identical
#			between the arms and this line is the whole
#			change.  tools/radio/pendtrial.py alternates them.
#

radio=cc1120 uid=2037303959315008002f0022
	board=clicker2 click=ccrf2 name=board1 call=N0CALL-1 peer=N0CALL-2
	role=caller
	pend=0
	# -3550ppb: a carrier from a generator calibrated by ear against
	# WWV appeared 3250Hz below where this part was commanded, at
	# both 915 and 910MHz, three runs identical.  With it applied
	# the same measurement gives +233Hz.
	ppb=-3550
	rssioffset=-102 csthr=-90
	# 0.930 from a tinySA attenuator cabled into the click, stepping
	# -52 to -76dBm in fours; 0.935 from this board's own PA ladder
	# driven from the host.  See doc/radio-plan.ms items 2 and 7.
	rssislope=0.930 slopesource=tinysa-attenuator

radio=cc1120 uid=203730395931500800300022
	board=clicker2 click=ccrf2 name=board2 call=N0CALL-2 peer=N0CALL-1
	role=answerer
	pend=0
	# +60300ppb, and this is why the file exists: it is 64ppm from
	# board 1's and no one kernel can carry both.  Measured by keying
	# each board into the same tinySA in one session so the
	# instrument's own error cancels in the difference; verified by
	# applying it, which moved this carrier +58798Hz against a
	# predicted +58442.
	ppb=60300
	rssioffset=-102 csthr=-90
	# 0.949 over 28dB, measured by radiocal(1) on the boards
	# themselves with board 1 transmitting: every packet's own RSSI
	# out of pkt's header rather than one reading a rung.
	rssislope=0.949 slopesource=radiocal-pa-ladder

#
# And two entries for qemu, which has no serial number of its own until
# it is given one.  tools/qemu-patches adds the system memory region the
# STM32F405 SoC never mapped -- without it a guest reading #P/uid takes a
# bus fault and dies in its init -- and makes the twelve bytes come from
# a "uid" property, so that two guests can be told apart.  The words are
# (U, U^0x5a5a5a5a, ~U) printed most significant first, so:
#
#	-global stm32f405-soc.uid=0x11111111
#	-global stm32f405-soc.uid=0x22222222
#
# Two guests that always answered alike could not exercise the check a
# two-board harness makes with the serial, which is that two boards
# reporting the same one are one board.
#
radio=cc1120 uid=eeeeeeee4b4b4b4b11111111
	board=qemu-netduinoplus2 click=cc1120-model name=qemu1
	call=N0CALL-9 peer=N0CALL-8 role=caller
	# The model's radio needs no crystal correction: it has no
	# crystal.  Left out rather than set to zero, so that a run which
	# expects a calibration says it did not find one.
	rssioffset=-102 csthr=-90

radio=cc1120 uid=dddddddd7878787822222222
	board=qemu-netduinoplus2 click=cc1120-model name=qemu2
	call=N0CALL-8 peer=N0CALL-9 role=answerer
	rssioffset=-102 csthr=-90
